export const HOME_FAQS = [
  { q: "What insurance does an industrial contractor need?", a: "Industrial contractors typically need general liability, high-hazard workers' compensation, contractors pollution liability (CPL), commercial auto, umbrella, tools & equipment coverage, and contractor bonds. Contractors working in refineries, chemical plants, or oil & gas facilities may also need industrial GL endorsements that address the specific hazards of those environments." },
  { q: "What is contractors pollution liability (CPL) and why do industrial contractors need it?", a: "CPL covers claims arising from pollution incidents during contractor operations — chemical spills, fuel releases, dust and fume exposure, and other pollution events that occur while your crews are working. Standard GL policies contain pollution exclusions that remove coverage for these events. For industrial contractors working in or around hazardous materials, CPL is essential." },
  { q: "How much does industrial contractor insurance cost?", a: "Cost depends on the type of industrial work (refinery, chemical plant, pipeline, manufacturing), crew size and payroll, OSHA hazard classification, tools and equipment value, annual revenue, and loss history. Industrial contractor programs are typically more expensive than standard commercial construction coverage because the hazards are higher. We quote your actual operation in about 15 minutes." },
  { q: "Does my standard GL cover work inside refineries and chemical plants?", a: "Often not fully. Standard commercial GL policies may exclude or limit coverage for operations inside industrial facilities, particularly those involving hazardous materials, confined spaces, or environmental pollution. Industrial GL endorsements and specialty markets provide coverage tailored to those environments." },
  { q: "What OSHA classifications matter for my workers' comp premium?", a: "Industrial labor is classified into specific workers' comp class codes based on the type of work — crane operation, ironworking, pipefitting, millwright work, plant maintenance mechanical, and many others. Each code carries a different rate per $100 of payroll, reflecting that trade's injury history. Correct classification is critical: wrong codes mean overpayment, undercoverage, and audit adjustments." },
  { q: "Can you insure industrial contractors with prior OSHA citations or loss history?", a: "Often yes. We have admitted and excess-and-surplus (E&S) markets for industrial contractors declined over OSHA citations, high-hazard classifications, or prior industrial loss runs. The key is presenting your safety program, remediation steps, and current practices accurately — underwriters value improvement as much as history." },
  { q: "What limits do industrial plant owners typically require?", a: "Requirements vary by plant owner and facility type, but commonly include $2M–$5M per occurrence GL, $5M–$25M umbrella, $1M CPL, and specific endorsements like waiver of subrogation and additional insured for the facility owner. We build programs specifically to meet facility-owner certificate requirements." },
  { q: "Does industrial contractor insurance cover confined space work?", a: "With the right program, yes. Confined space operations carry heightened injury risk (atmospheric hazards, engulfment, rescue requirements) and require GL and workers' comp that accounts for that exposure. Standard policies may not adequately rate or cover confined space work — we specify it correctly." },
  { q: "Are crane operators and riggers covered under standard workers' comp?", a: "Crane operators and riggers have specific high-hazard workers' comp class codes that reflect their injury risk. If they're misclassified under generic contractor or maintenance codes, your premium may be wrong and your coverage may be disputed at claim time. We classify crane and rigging operations correctly." },
  { q: "What is industrial GL and how is it different from standard GL?", a: "Industrial general liability is GL specifically endorsed and underwritten for the hazards of industrial facility work — hot work permits, LOTO (lockout/tagout), confined space, chemical exposure, and other industrial hazards. It typically includes broader pollution coverage, higher limits, and specific endorsements that plant owners require on contractor certificates." },
  { q: "Does CPL cover claims from work performed inside a plant or refinery?", a: "Yes. Contractors Pollution Liability covers pollution incidents that arise from your operations — whether at a refinery, chemical plant, pipeline, or other industrial site. It covers third-party bodily injury, property damage, and cleanup costs from pollution events during or after your work." },
  { q: "Are tools and heavy equipment covered on industrial job sites?", a: "Tools and equipment insurance covers your portable tools, specialized equipment, scaffolding, and rigging gear against theft, damage, and breakdown — on and off the job site. For industrial contractors with significant equipment investment, it's a critical coverage that standard property policies often exclude for off-premises equipment." },
  { q: "Do you write industrial contractor insurance in all 50 states?", a: "Yes. Contractors Choice Agency is licensed in all 50 states and has markets for industrial contractors whether your crews work in Texas, Louisiana, Ohio, California, or anywhere in the country." },
  { q: "How fast can we get an industrial contractor insurance quote?", a: "Typically 15 minutes on a call. For larger programs or highly hazardous classifications, we may need a day or two to involve the right specialty markets, but we move fast and tell you the timeline up front." },
  { q: "What is a waiver of subrogation and why do plant owners require it?", a: "A waiver of subrogation means your insurer agrees not to sue the facility owner to recover costs after a claim — even if the owner was partially at fault. Industrial plant owners require it to protect themselves from claims brought by contractor insurers after workplace incidents. We add it to your GL and workers' comp as required." },
  { q: "Does industrial contractor insurance cover work during plant turnarounds and shutdowns?", a: "Yes. Plant turnarounds and shutdowns are peak-exposure periods — large crews in concentrated spaces performing high-hazard maintenance work. We build programs that reflect turnaround exposure, including any temporary surge in payroll and the compressed timeline of intensive industrial work." },
  { q: "What should I provide for an industrial contractor insurance quote?", a: "Types of industrial work performed, facility types (refinery, chemical plant, manufacturing), typical crew size and payroll, OSHA classification history, tools and equipment value, revenue by work type, current coverage, and loss history for the past 5 years. The more detail, the more accurate the quote." },
  { q: "Can industrial contractors get umbrella coverage for the limits plant owners require?", a: "Yes. Our commercial umbrella programs can reach the $10M–$25M limits that many industrial plant owners and energy companies require on contractor certificates. Umbrella coverage is also far more cost-effective than trying to purchase that level of primary GL." },
  { q: "Are industrial contractors covered if a chemical release injures workers or damages property?", a: "With both workers' comp and CPL in place, yes. Workers' comp covers injured employees; CPL covers third-party bodily injury, property damage, and cleanup costs from the pollution event. Without CPL, the standard GL pollution exclusion leaves you exposed for the environmental and third-party side of a chemical release." },
  { q: "Should I bundle all my industrial coverage with one program?", a: "Usually yes. A coordinated program closes gaps between GL, workers' comp, CPL, and auto — and is almost always cheaper and cleaner than separate policies from separate carriers, especially when plant owners require multiple lines on a single certificate." },
];

